{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1410589754,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU1NDgzNDI4","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1#issuecomment-55483428"},"message":"Implemented: http://git-annex.branchable.com/devblog/day_219__catching_up_and_looking_back/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1411655879,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODI4MDU5","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1#issuecomment-56828059"},"message":"as it was already implemented for the original use-case (for the same fs supporting hardlinks), closing","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1411655879,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTcwMTk4MTYx"},"status":2},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1411656136,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3MDIwMDk4Nw=="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1411656138,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3MDIwMDk5Nw=="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1421839761,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDcwODIyMjY2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1#issuecomment-70822266"},"message":"I am reopening this one again. Here is a scenario that I feel is poorly supported right now -- maybe that is the local optimum already, but maybe it could be done in a different way.\n\nImagine a webserver exposing a git annex repo (A) via http. I have a collaborator that needs to get access to additional unpublished parts of a new revision of the dataset in (A). My approach is to go to the webserver and\n1. `git clone --shared` (A) into (B)\n2. merge the branch with the new bits from a remote into (B)\n3. `git annex get .`\n\nThe last step will hardlink files in the annex of (A) when possible and copy the rest from the remote of (B) into (B). Now I should be be able to give the HTTP URL of (B) to my collaborator and git annex should do the rest for them.\n\nHowever, the problem is that, most likely, they cannot clone (B) via http. Git assumes with `--shared` that it can obtain objects from the `.git` of (A) when cloning (B) via the same access method. Example:\n\n(B) is cloned locally from /media/data/orig from (A) and is exposed via http://BASEURL/t002\n(C) is cloned from (B) via http://BASEURL/t002\n\nThis fails at the git clone stage, because git requests objects from (A) via http://BASEURL/media/data/orig (remote URL and local path concatenated).\n\nIn order to fix this, the webserver needs to get an Alias definition that makes such URLs valid.\n\nLong story short: It would be good to have a switch that enables git-annex annex hard-linking without the implications `git clone --shared`. Actually, the ideal solution seems to be the combination of git's behavior with `clone --local` and what git annex is doing now with `clone --shared`.\n\n@joeyh Does this make sense, or am I approaching it wrong?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1469020727,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIzMzk0NjA5NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2#issuecomment-233946094"},"message":"Sample projects to work on\n- big project:  ABIDE on NITRC-IR  http://www.nitrc.org/ir/app/action/DisplayItemAction/search_element/xnat%3AprojectData/search_field/xnat%3AprojectData.ID/search_value/ABIDE","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"e714e3eba03a07969571eec2d03a569d212f3cf8"},"timestamp":1561919466,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUwNzA1NzEzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2#issuecomment-507057134"},"message":"@yarikoptic : I